    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a serious plateau on their back squat. they haven't added any weight in 3 weeks and their RPE is hitting 10 way too early. can you explain how deload weeks and changing up the volume might help them break through this? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Deload weeks are periods of reduced training intensity or volume, allowing the body to recover and preventing overtraining. Introducing a deload week can help your client recover from the plateau and build strength back up. Changing up the volume, such as varying workout frequency, implementing drop sets, or incorporating different exercises, might also help eliminate the plateaus. Adjustments help to shock the neuromuscular system and could push past the current limit. If the plateau persists, reassessing nutrition, sleep, and consistency in training would also be beneficial.",
